---
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/Lunatico0/lunatic-battery';
const maintainer = 'https://github.com/Lunatico0';
const privacyHref = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}privacy/`;
---

<LegalPage title="Code signing policy" updated="2026-11-26">
  <p class="legal-callout">
    Free code signing provided by <a href="https://signpath.io">SignPath.io</a>, certificate by <a href="https://signpath.org">SignPath Foundation</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What gets signed</h2>
  <p>
    Lunatic Battery is a free, open source Windows tray app (MIT license). Only binaries built from the public source
    code in <a href={repo}>github.com/Lunatico0/lunatic-battery</a> are signed: the installer and the app files of each
    release published on the <a href={`${repo}/releases`}>Releases page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How a release is built</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every release is built by GitHub Actions from the public repository, with the workflow
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/HEAD/.github/workflows/release.yml`}><code>.github/workflows/release.yml</code></a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The workflow runs only when a version tag (<code>v*</code>) is pushed. It runs the test suite, builds the app and
      uploads the result as a draft release.
    </li>
    <li>Each signing request is approved manually by the maintainer before the release is signed.</li>
    <li>Until the SignPath Foundation application is approved, releases are unsigned.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Team roles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Committers and reviewers: <a href={maintainer}>Patricio Pittana</a></li>
    <li>Approvers: <a href={maintainer}>Patricio Pittana</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The project has a single maintainer, who reviews every change before it is merged.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    Lunatic Battery has no telemetry, no analytics and no accounts. The only information it transfers to another
    networked system on its own is its update check:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An installed copy contacts GitHub to look for a newer release of
      <a href={repo}>Lunatico0/lunatic-battery</a>: one minute after the app starts, then every 6 hours while it runs,
      and whenever the user picks "Buscar actualizaciones" (Check for updates) in the tray menu.
    </li>
    <li>
      These are normal HTTPS requests to GitHub. GitHub sees your IP address, as with any download. The app sends no
      personal data, device names, battery readings or settings.
    </li>
    <li>
      When a newer release exists, it is downloaded from GitHub in the background and installed when the app exits or restarts.
    </li>
    <li>
      There is no setting to turn the automatic check off. To stop it, block the app in your firewall: the battery
      display keeps working, it just stops updating.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Apart from that, the app only opens a web page when you click a link in it, and its diagnostic report stays on
    your PC unless you copy or save it and share it yourself. The full details are in the
    <a href={privacyHref}>privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
